@*
    Hidden fields every comment form carries (see Blog.Core.Services.CommentSpamFilter and ADR-0031).

    - __RequestVerificationToken: the anti-forgery token.
    - commentToken: the form's signed issue time. Proves the form was fetched and tells the filter how long the
      reader spent writing.
    On a post page both slots are empty, so the page sets no cookie and can be cached: inkwell-comments.js fetches
    them from /{slug}/comment-token when the comments come near the screen. The respond page (no JavaScript)
    renders them filled, through ViewData["CommentRequestToken"] and ViewData["CommentFormToken"].
    - contact_ref: a honeypot. Off-screen, unlabeled, excluded from the tab order and from autofill;
      a real reader never sees it, so anything posted in it is a form-filling script.

    Layout-independent by design: inline styles only, no CSS class any of the 20 layouts must define.
*@
@{
    var requestToken = ViewData["CommentRequestToken"] as string ?? "";
    var commentFormToken = ViewData["CommentFormToken"] as string ?? "";
}
<input type="hidden" name="__RequestVerificationToken" value="@requestToken" data-comment-request-token />
<input type="hidden" name="@Blog.Web.Services.CommentFormTokenService.FieldName" value="@commentFormToken" data-comment-form-token />
<div aria-hidden="true" style="position:absolute;inset-inline-start:-10000px;top:auto;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;">
    <label>Leave this field empty <input type="text" name="@Blog.Web.Services.CommentFormTokenService.HoneypotFieldName" value="" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" /></label>
</div>
